  "context": {
    "axis": "can committed chi witnesses be used to extend an A-scan past A = 4?",
    "answer": "Not directly. Four conventions are in use and only sign-only-crossings reaches n = 15 and 18, while the binding law's calibration atoms are colour-weighted-averaged-crossing. Walking from A = 4 to A = 5 on committed witnesses changes convention at the boundary, and any shape change there would be an artefact.",
    "what_is_safe": "y_lambda is convention-independent (agrees to 1e-51), because the Markov weights depend on n and q alone. chi_lambda is not (differs by 9.46 at n = 6 and 305.15 at n = 12, same atom). So the weights may be lifted and only the characters need recomputing — which is what makes extending the scan tractable.",
    "scope": "Scope-(a). Nothing refuted, no witness edited. The convention field is present and correct everywhere; the hazard is that nothing forces a consumer to compare it."
  },
